WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.080
Sometimes people think of
a large language model

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.380
as a knowledge store, as if it's
learned to memorize a

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.781
lot of information, maybe
off the internet, so

4
00:00:12.781 --> 00:00:16.081
when you ask it a question, it
can answer the question.

5
00:00:16.722 --> 00:00:20.192
But I think a even more useful way
to think of a

6
00:00:20.192 --> 00:00:22.795
large language model is
sometimes as a reasoning engine,

7
00:00:22.795 --> 00:00:26.265
in which you can give it chunks of
text or other sources

8
00:00:26.265 --> 00:00:26.844
of information.

9
00:00:27.365 --> 00:00:30.726
And then the large language model,
LLM, will maybe use

10
00:00:30.726 --> 00:00:33.751
this background knowledge that's
learned off the internet, but

11
00:00:33.751 --> 00:00:36.440
to use the new
information you give it

12
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.129
to help you answer
questions or reason through

13
00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:42.827
content or decide even
what to do next. And that's what

14
00:00:42.827 --> 00:00:45.180
LangChain's Agents framework
helps you to do.

15
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:45.180


16
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.411
Agents are probably my
favorite part of LangChain.

17
00:00:47.411 --> 00:00:50.480
I think they're
also one of the most powerful parts, but

18
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.990
they're also
one of the newer parts. So we're

19
00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:56.058
seeing a lot of stuff emerge
here that's really new to

20
00:00:56.058 --> 00:00:59.685
everyone in the field. And so this should
be a very exciting lesson

21
00:00:59.685 --> 00:01:02.753
as we dive into what
agents are, how to create and

22
00:01:02.753 --> 00:01:06.101
how to use agents, how to equip them
with different types of

23
00:01:06.101 --> 00:01:08.611
tools, like search engines that
come built into LangChain,

24
00:01:08.611 --> 00:01:10.843
and then also how
to create your own

25
00:01:10.843 --> 00:01:13.632
tools, so that you can
let agents interact with any

26
00:01:13.632 --> 00:01:15.864
data stores, any APIs, any
functions that you

27
00:01:15.864 --> 00:01:16.980
might want them to.


28
00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.074
So this is exciting, cutting-edge
stuff, but

29
00:01:20.074 --> 00:01:24.495
already with emerging, important
use cases. So with that, let's

30
00:01:24.495 --> 00:01:25.380
dive in!

31
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:30.458
So to get started with Agents, we're
going to start as we always do, by

32
00:01:30.458 --> 00:01:32.151
importing the correct
environment variables.

33
00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:36.112
We're also going to need to
install a few packages here. So

34
00:01:36.112 --> 00:01:39.247
we're going to use the "duckduckgo-search" engine
and "wikipedia". So we're

35
00:01:39.247 --> 00:01:42.381
going to want
to pip install those. I've already installed those

36
00:01:42.381 --> 00:01:45.231
in my environment, so I'm not going
to run this

37
00:01:45.231 --> 00:01:48.936
line. But if you guys have not,
you should uncomment that line, run

38
00:01:48.936 --> 00:01:50.930
it, and then you're good
to go.

39
00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:50.930


40
00:01:51.672 --> 00:01:54.325
We're then going to import
some methods and classes

41
00:01:54.325 --> 00:01:57.862
that we need from LangChain. So
we're going to import some methods

42
00:01:57.862 --> 00:01:58.747
to load tools.

43
00:01:59.308 --> 00:02:01.989
And these are things that we're
going to connect the

44
00:02:01.989 --> 00:02:04.671
language model to. We're going
to load a method to

45
00:02:04.671 --> 00:02:07.352
initialize the agent. We're going to
load the Chat OpenAI

46
00:02:07.352 --> 00:02:09.229
wrapper, and we're going to load
"AgentType".

47
00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:11.982
So "AgentType"
will be used to specify what

48
00:02:11.982 --> 00:02:15.544
type of agent we want to use.
There are a bunch of different

49
00:02:15.544 --> 00:02:18.558
types of agents in LangChain. We're
not going to go over

50
00:02:18.558 --> 00:02:22.668
all of them right now. We'll just
choose one and run with that. We're then

51
00:02:22.668 --> 00:02:25.682
going to initialize the language model
that we're going to use.

52
00:02:25.682 --> 00:02:28.422
Again, we're using this as the
reasoning engine that we're

53
00:02:28.422 --> 00:02:29.792
using to drive the agent.


54
00:02:31.235 --> 00:02:33.439
We'll then load the tools that we're going
to use.

55
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.932
So we're going to load DuckDuckGo
search and Wikipedia. And

56
00:02:37.932 --> 00:02:41.507
these are built-in LangChain
tools. Finally, we're going to

57
00:02:41.507 --> 00:02:42.699
initialize the agent.

58
00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.328
We pass it the tools,
the language model, and agent type.

59
00:02:47.328 --> 00:02:47.328


60
00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:51.036
And so here we're using "CHAT_ZERO_SHOT_REACT_DESCRIPTION".


61
00:02:51.858 --> 00:02:54.940
Not going to go over in
too much detail what this means.

62
00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.765
The important things to note are chat.
This is optimized to

63
00:02:57.765 --> 00:02:58.792
work with chat models.

64
00:02:59.413 --> 00:03:02.728
And then react. React is a
prompting strategy that

65
00:03:02.728 --> 00:03:05.306
elicits better thoughts
from a language model.

66
00:03:05.987 --> 00:03:08.853
We're also going to set "handle_parsing_errors=True".


67
00:03:09.615 --> 00:03:12.836
If you remember from the first lesson,
we chatted a bunch

68
00:03:12.836 --> 00:03:16.349
about output parsers and
how those can be used to take the

69
00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:19.277
LLM output, which is a string,
and parse it into

70
00:03:19.277 --> 00:03:21.620
a specific format that
we can use downstream.

71
00:03:22.321 --> 00:03:26.206
That's extremely important here. When
we take the output of the LLM, which

72
00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:29.791
is text, and parse it into
the specific action and the specific

73
00:03:29.791 --> 00:03:32.182
action input that
the language model should take.

74
00:03:33.184 --> 00:03:37.345
Let's now use this agent. Let's ask
it a question about a recent

75
00:03:37.345 --> 00:03:41.505
event that the model, when it
was trained, didn't know. So let's ask

76
00:03:41.505 --> 00:03:43.425
it about
the 2022 World Cup.

77
00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:47.529
The models here were trained
on data up to around 2021.

78
00:03:47.529 --> 00:03:50.495
So it shouldn't
know the answer to this question. And

79
00:03:50.495 --> 00:03:53.460
so it should realize that
it needs to use a

80
00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.130
tool to look up this
recent piece of information.

81
00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:07.961
So we can see here that the agent
realizes that it needs

82
00:04:07.961 --> 00:04:11.695
to use DuckDuckGo search,
and then looks up

83
00:04:11.695 --> 00:04:14.028
the 2022 World Cup winner.

84
00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:19.109
And so it gets back a bunch of
information. We can then see

85
00:04:19.109 --> 00:04:21.643
that the agent thinks
that the 2022 World

86
00:04:21.643 --> 00:04:23.227
Cup has not happened yet.

87
00:04:24.008 --> 00:04:28.029
So this is a good example of why
agents are still pretty exploratory.

88
00:04:28.029 --> 00:04:31.122
You can see that there's
a bunch of information here

89
00:04:31.122 --> 00:04:33.906
about the 2022 World Cup, but
it doesn't quite

90
00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:38.546
realize all that has happened. And so
it needs to look up more things and

91
00:04:38.546 --> 00:04:42.258
get more information. And so
then based on that information, it can

92
00:04:42.258 --> 00:04:44.732
respond with the correct
answer that "Argentina won

93
00:04:44.732 --> 00:04:45.970
the 2022 World Cup".

94
00:04:47.495 --> 00:04:49.873
Let's then ask it a
question where it should

95
00:04:49.873 --> 00:04:51.723
recognize that it needs
to use Wikipedia.

96
00:04:52.585 --> 00:04:56.292
So Wikipedia, it has a lot
of information about specific

97
00:04:56.292 --> 00:05:00.370
people and specific entities
from a long time ago. It doesn't

98
00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.224
need to be current information.

99
00:05:03.066 --> 00:05:05.591
So let's ask it about Tom M. Mitchell,
an

100
00:05:05.591 --> 00:05:08.396
American computer scientist. And
what book did he write? We

101
00:05:08.396 --> 00:05:10.641
can see here that
it recognizes that it

102
00:05:10.641 --> 00:05:13.166
should use Wikipedia to
look up the answer. So

103
00:05:13.166 --> 00:05:15.129
it searches for Tom M. Mitchell
Wikipedia.

104
00:05:16.111 --> 00:05:19.429
And then does another follow-up
search just to make sure that it's got

105
00:05:19.429 --> 00:05:22.235
the right answer. So it
searches for "Tom M. Mitchell Machine

106
00:05:22.235 --> 00:05:23.767
Learning" and gets
back more information.

107
00:05:24.448 --> 00:05:27.962
And then based on that, it's
able to finally answer with "Tom M. Mitchell

108
00:05:27.962 --> 00:05:29.217
wrote the textbook, 'Machine
Learning'".

109
00:05:29.738 --> 00:05:31.996
You should pause the
video here and try

110
00:05:31.996 --> 00:05:33.125
putting in different inputs.

111
00:05:33.726 --> 00:05:37.478
So far, we've used tools that come
defined in LangChain already, but

112
00:05:37.478 --> 00:05:41.229
a big power of agents is that you
can connect it to

113
00:05:41.229 --> 00:05:44.668
your own sources of information, your
own APIs, your own data.

114
00:05:45.209 --> 00:05:47.156
So here we're going to go over
how you can create

115
00:05:47.156 --> 00:05:48.572
a custom tool so
that you can connect

116
00:05:48.572 --> 00:05:49.457
it to whatever you want.

117
00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.063
Let's make a tool that's going
to tell us what the

118
00:05:53.063 --> 00:05:53.686
current data is.

119
00:05:54.788 --> 00:05:57.072
First, we're
going to import this tool decorator.

120
00:05:57.072 --> 00:05:57.072


121
00:05:57.593 --> 00:05:59.979
This can be applied
to any function and

122
00:05:59.979 --> 00:06:03.260
it turns it into
a tool that LangChain can use. Next,

123
00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.646
we're
going to write a function called "time",

124
00:06:05.646 --> 00:06:10.119
which takes in any text string. We're
not really going to use that. And it's

125
00:06:10.119 --> 00:06:12.804
going to return today's date by calling
date time.

126
00:06:15.629 --> 00:06:19.105
In addition to the name of the function,
we're also going to write a

127
00:06:19.105 --> 00:06:20.098
really detailed doc string.

128
00:06:20.699 --> 00:06:23.096
That's because this is what
the agent will use

129
00:06:23.096 --> 00:06:26.291
to know when it
should call this tool and how it should

130
00:06:26.291 --> 00:06:27.090
call this tool.

131
00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:30.444
For example, here we say that,
"The input should always

132
00:06:30.444 --> 00:06:33.088
be an empty string.". That's
because we don't use it.

133
00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:36.572
If we have more
stringent requirements on what

134
00:06:36.572 --> 00:06:39.826
the input should be, for example,
if we have a

135
00:06:39.826 --> 00:06:43.730
function that should always
take in a search query or a SQL

136
00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:47.634
statement, you'll want to make sure
to mention that here. We're now

137
00:06:47.634 --> 00:06:50.887
going to create another agent. This time
we're adding the

138
00:06:50.887 --> 00:06:53.490
time tool to the list
of existing tools.

139
00:06:55.753 --> 00:06:58.361
And finally, let's
call the agent and ask it

140
00:06:58.361 --> 00:06:59.810
what the date today is.

141
00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.601
It recognizes that it needs
to use the time tool,

142
00:07:06.601 --> 00:07:07.717
which it specifies here.

143
00:07:08.299 --> 00:07:10.523
It has the action input as an empty
string. This is great.

144
00:07:10.523 --> 00:07:12.006
This is what we told it to do.

145
00:07:12.647 --> 00:07:16.391
And then it returns with an observation.
And then finally,

146
00:07:16.391 --> 00:07:18.637
the language model
takes that observation

147
00:07:18.637 --> 00:07:22.006
and responds to the user, "Today's
date is 2023-05-21".

148
00:07:22.627 --> 00:07:25.395
You should pause the
video here and try

149
00:07:25.395 --> 00:07:29.201
putting in different inputs.
This wraps up the lesson on agents.

150
00:07:29.201 --> 00:07:32.315
This is one of the newer
and more exciting

151
00:07:32.315 --> 00:07:34.391
and more experimental
pieces of LangChain.

152
00:07:34.892 --> 00:07:38.638
So I hope you enjoy using it.
Hopefully it showed you how you

153
00:07:38.638 --> 00:07:41.232
